I'm Callie a reading for reviewer  Richard way cling code vain establishes  its own identity from the outset it may  latch on to a Dark Souls formula that  has come to define a generation of  action RPGs but it manages to set itself  apart from the rest by presenting a  post-apocalyptic world filled with what  are essentially anime vampires  [Music]  interesting concepts and mechanics  filter out from the central blood-soaked  idea resulting in a game that feels  familiar yet wildly different from the  souls games that inspired it yet it's  the parts that are most recognizable  such as its combat work code fate and  stumbles after an apocalyptic Cataclysm  ravages the world those who died are  brought back to life as immortal beings  called revenants the price they have to  pay for reincarnation is an insatiable  thirst for blood  fortunately a revenant don't have to  feed on the few remaining humans to  survive blood beads grow on plants  throughout the world and function as  suitable substitutes for human blood  nourishing a revenant bloodlust in much  the same way the problem is blood beads  are becoming increasingly sparse so it's  up to you to find the source and  hopefully attain a steady supply in  stereotypical protagonist fashion you  begin the game by waking up with amnesia  before finding out you're the chosen one  you see each revenant and code vein has  a class known as a blood code your  created character is special because  they're not confined to a single blood  code like everyone else's this  malleability allows you to swap between  various classes whenever you feel like  it with your arsenal of available blood  codes expanding the further you progress  throughout the game blood codes are  tailored to a specific style of play  that often fits into a typical RPG class  template think warrior or mage gifts are  code veins version of abilities granting  you access to a wide range of passive  and active skills that are tied but not  limited to each Blood code you're able  to mix and match gifts to a certain  degree with the most exciting ones  letting you unleash flashy special  attacks in melee combat others allow you  to fire projectiles of piercing Ike or  boost your attack power or temporarily  add a stun effect to your weapon and  there are many more the character  creator is already comprehensive enough  but code Vayne provides a plethora of  options when it comes to finding a play  style that suits you incorporating gifts  amid regular attacks makes for some  satisfying combos and there's a  gratifying heft behind each slash and  crunch of code Bane's melee combat  each gift consumes from a pool of I core  that's replenished by simply defeating  enemies or refilled in larger doses by  performing drain attacks parries and  back steps this incentivizes you to use  gifts regularly approaching each enemy  with an offensive mindset twin leash a  bevy of special attacks and then quickly  regain any lost I core wailing on  enemies is satisfying and gifts spruce  up each fight with their inherent  flexibility yet combat is a  disappointingly by-the-numbers affair  because of the AI shortcomings there's  an adequate variety of enemy types but  this variety generally only applies to  their visual design as opposed to their  behavior and movesets  they're surprisingly static spending  most of their time simply idling instead  of reacting to your attacks each weapon  you wield is usually able to stagger  enemies on the first or second hit  allowing you to dispatch each foe with  almost no resistance and this remains  true throughout the entirety of the game  there are a few enemies that break away  from this mould requiring you to  actually dodge and make use of your  gifts but they're an anomaly amid a sea  of one-sided slugfest bosses aren't  quite as easy to take down but they're  not far from it there are no patterns to  learn or delicate back-and-forth setting  compass every aspect of code veins  combat it's simple enough to be each  boss on your first your second attempt  by simply maneuvering behind them this  only deviates as you approach the end  credits and bosses receive a sudden  difficulty spike as they rely on  powerful area-of-effect tax and homing  projectiles code Vayne doesn't have to  adhere darksouls challenging difficulty  but it also misses the mark by never  forcing you to learn or deepen your  understanding of the game to progress  combat devolves into a mindless task or  the only thing you need to watch out for  is enemy placement and quantity  exploring each environment is engaging  at least the level design has a tendency  to wrap in and around itself offering  secret paths and capturing the elation  that's derived from opening a shortcut  or discovering a new checkpoint to rest  at one sprawling area even borrows an  Orlando's distinct Ile Duomo inspired  aesthetic reimagining the pearly white  castle as a labyrinth in maze it's just  a shame the visual design is regularly  pedestrian you spend the vast majority  of your time traversing through bland  post-apocalyptic streets and damp caves  were a rumble as code veins most  distinguishing feature the  of fire and sand shakes up the typical  dilapidated cityscape but it's not  nearly enough to shake the feeling that  you've seen it all before there's even a  late area that adopts the anor londo  aesthetic for a second time the only  difference is that now it's inside and  slightly darker moving through an area  that conjures memories of Dark Souls  most memorable location doesn't do code  vain any favors it adopts the souls like  formula and its structure presenting a  familiar cycle of progression and basic  combat similarities and there are some  interesting ideas here - built around  the use of various blood codes and their  distinct gifts you can see the fragments  of a fantastic game hidden within these  systems and it's meaty combat feedback  but the mundanity of its enemies and the  effect they have on nullifying the  combats enjoyment prevents code Vayne  from ever realizing it's potential  you  [Music]
 

